verdict.corrected_fix:
Fix the misspelling and add the missing headers, using the `always` flag so they are sent on error responses too. Because nginx does not inherit server-level add_header into a location that sets its own, declare them where they must apply (either promote to the server block AND repeat inside `location /`, or move Cache-Control up). Example at server level plus repeated in `location /`:
add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains" always;
add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;
add_header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; ..." always;
Note that HSTS only takes effect over HTTPS, so also confirm TLS is terminated at this container or an upstream edge and that the edge does not already own the HSTS header.

Apply formula neutralization to every header and data cell before writing, in generate_cohort_csv and generate_pivottable_csv (and to header_names in generate_cohort_json). Caveat on the proposed helper: blindly prefixing any value whose first char is '-' will turn legitimate negative numbers (common in lab/clinical data) into text strings and corrupt the dataset. Refine so numeric-looking values are left intact, e.g.:

def _csv_safe(v):
    s = '' if v is None else str(v)
    if not s:
        return s
    if s[0] in ('=', '+', '@', '\t', '\r'):
        return "'" + s
    if s[0] == '-':
        # only neutralize if it is NOT a plain negative number
        try:
            float(s)
        except ValueError:
            return "'" + s
    return s

Use `writer.writerow([_csv_safe(c) for c in header_names])` and `writer.writerow([_csv_safe(c) for c in row])`. For the pivot table, stop the naive `dataset.split('\n')` / `row.split(',')` parsing entirely: obtain the DataFrame from run_analysis and write it with pandas (df.to_csv(response, index=..., quoting=csv.QUOTE_MINIMAL)) after applying _csv_safe to string cells, so comma-containing values are correctly quoted.

Correct the typo and set a proper value, and place the headers where they are actually emitted. Because the `location /` block defines its own add_header (dropping inherited ones) and error responses need coverage, use the `always` flag and repeat the security headers inside `location /` (or move all of them into a shared snippet included in both). Example inside the location that serves index.html:
add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains" always;
add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;
add_header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" always;
add_header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'" always;
Note that HSTS is only honored on HTTPS responses, so ensure TLS is terminated at or in front of this server before relying on it. Test the CSP against the app's actual script/style/connect origins (Vite build, /api backend) before enforcing.
